      "code": "import Mathlib\n\nstructure VerifiableStack (T : Type) where\n  data : List T\n\ndef VerifiableStack.view {T} (s : VerifiableStack T) : List T :=\n  s.data\n\ndef VerifiableStack.is_valid {T} (s : VerifiableStack T) : Prop :=\n  True\n\n-- Precondition definitions\n@[reducible, simp]\ndef pop_precond {T} (s : VerifiableStack T) : Prop :=\n  -- !benchmark @start precond\n  s.is_valid \u2227 s.view.length > 0\n  -- !benchmark @end precond\n\n-- !benchmark @start auxcode\ntheorem VerifiableStack.data_ne_nil {T} (s : VerifiableStack T)\n    (h : s.is_valid \u2227 s.view.length > 0) : s.data \u2260 [] := by\n  intro hnil\n  have hlen := h.2\n  simp [VerifiableStack.view, hnil] at hlen\n-- !benchmark @end auxcode\n\n-- Main function definition\n-- Returns the projected pair (val, new_stack)\ndef pop {T} (s : VerifiableStack T)\n    (h_precond : pop_precond s) : T \u00d7 VerifiableStack T :=\n  -- !benchmark @start code\n  (s.data.getLast (VerifiableStack.data_ne_nil s h_precond),\n   \u27e8s.data.dropLast\u27e9)\n  -- !benchmark @end code\n\n-- Postcondition definitions\n@[reducible, simp]\ndef pop_postcond {T} (s : VerifiableStack T) (result : T \u00d7 VerifiableStack T)\n    (_ : pop_precond s) : Prop := -- We don't even need the proof term 'h' here!\n  -- !benchmark @start postcond\n  let (val, new_stack) := result\n  new_stack.is_valid \u2227\n  -- Concise & Idiomatic: The old stack was the new stack followed by the popped value.\n  s.view = new_stack.view ++ [val]\n  -- !benchmark @end postcond\n\n-- !benchmark @start lemma\n-- !benchmark @end lemma\n\n-- Proof content\ntheorem pop_postcond_satisfied {T} (s : VerifiableStack T)\n    (h_precond : pop_precond s) :\n    pop_postcond s (pop s h_precond) h_precond := by\n  -- !benchmark @start proof\n  refine \u27e8trivial, ?_\u27e9\n  simp only [VerifiableStack.view]\n  exact (List.dropLast_append_getLast (VerifiableStack.data_ne_nil s h_precond)).symm\n  -- !benchmark @end proof\n",
      "comment": "Done \u2014 `Solution.lean` verifies with no errors and no `sorry` (2 compiler checks used).\n\n**Reasoning.** The stack's `view` is its `data` list, and the postcondition `s.view = new_stack.view ++ [val]` places the top of the stack at the *end* of the list. So `pop` is:\n\n- popped value = `s.data.getLast _`\n- new stack = `\u27e8s.data.dropLast\u27e9`\n\n**What I added in each marker region:**\n\n- **auxcode** \u2014 `VerifiableStack.data_ne_nil`, turning the precondition `s.view.length > 0` into the `s.data \u2260 []` proof that `List.getLast` demands. Sharing it between the definition and the proof keeps the two uses of the non-emptiness witness definitionally identical, so the proof doesn't have to reconcile two different proof terms.\n- **code** \u2014 the pair `(s.data.getLast (data_ne_nil s h_precond), \u27e8s.data.dropLast\u27e9)`. It is computable and total: the proof argument is exactly the safety precondition the task asked to be threaded through.\n- **lemma** \u2014 left empty; Mathlib already has what's needed.\n- **proof** \u2014 `is_valid` is `True`, so the first conjunct is `trivial`; the second unfolds `view` and is precisely `List.dropLast_append_getLast` reversed.\n\nThe one remaining warning (`unused variable 's'` at line 9) comes from the given `is_valid` definition, which I left untouched as required."
